// String interpolation keeps every slot rooted across a collection that happens
// while a LATER slot is still being built (#1409).
//
// Darwin-only by construction, hence the sibling `darwin-only` marker: the
// reported shape involves an `extern function` (SPEC §11.7), which the compiler
// rejects on both Linux targets with E0078 — Bit's ELF output is fully static,
// so an extern symbol there has nothing to resolve against.
//
// WHAT THIS PINS. `lowerStrInterp` converts EVERY part to a string first and
// only then joins them — through `string_concat5`, four new parts per call, since
// #4037, and through the binary `string_concat` before that. Either way, while
// part N is still being evaluated, the strings for parts 0..N-1 are live values
// with no name in the source — they exist only as SSA temporaries. If the
// collector fails to see one of them, it is swept, its 16-byte `RtBytes` header
// is handed to the very next allocation, and the earlier slot prints whatever
// that later allocation wrote. The failure is silent: no crash, no diagnostic,
// just a wrong line. A `bool` slot is the sharpest witness, because a recycled
// header makes `true` print as a number — the slot appears to have lost its
// TYPE, not merely its value.
//
// WHY THE ORACLE IS ACTUALLY LIVE HERE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT. `BIT_GC=stress`
// collects at every safepoint, but codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` only at loop
// back-edges (runtime/ABI.md §4) — `bit_rt_alloc` does not poll. A straight-line
// program therefore performs ZERO collections no matter how much it allocates,
// and running it under `BIT_GC=stress` proves nothing at all. That is why the
// middle slots call `churn`, which owns a loop: its back-edge is a real poll, so
// a collection is forced at a point where slots 0..N-1 are live and unnamed.
// Without that call this file would pass whether or not the roots were reported,
// which is exactly the trap it exists to close. _tests_/stress/gcbit's counter
// confirms the collections happen rather than assuming they do.
//
// `churn` also allocates enough to hand a swept header straight back out, so a
// missed root shows up as corrupted output rather than staying latent.

// Declared `i32` because that is what C's `pid_t` actually is. Widening the
// declaration to `i64` would leave the upper half of the return register
// undefined at the C-ABI boundary — the same class of silent wrongness that
// made `!fsExists(missing)` false on x86_64 — and this file must not depend on
// a bug it is not testing.
extern fn getpid(): i32

// A loop, therefore a safepoint at its back-edge, therefore a collection under
// BIT_GC=stress. Returns `n` so the printed line stays deterministic.
fn churn(n: int): int {
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    let junk = []int(8)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return n
}

// Genuinely derived from an `extern function` result, and not foldable: the
// optimizer cannot know that two `getpid()` calls agree, so the division
// survives to run time even though its value is always 1.
fn externOne(): int {
  return int(getpid()) / int(getpid())
}

fn main() {
  let t = true
  let f = false
  let s = "S"

  // Slots 0-2 are built, then `churn` forces a collection while they are live
  // and unnamed, then an extern-derived slot allocates, then the SAME bool and
  // string values are printed again. The two halves must agree: a swept slot
  // corrupts the first occurrence while the second — reached after the sweep —
  // still reads its variable, so a rooting hole shows as a MISMATCH between
  // them, not merely as an odd-looking line.
  print("${t} ${f} ${s} ${churn(40)} ${externOne()} ${t} ${f} ${s}\n")

  // The same shape with the extern-derived slot FIRST, so the value that is
  // live across the collection is itself extern-derived rather than a literal.
  print("${externOne()} ${t} ${churn(40)} ${s} ${f} ${externOne()}\n")

  // Many slots, each separated by a forced collection, so a hole that only
  // appears once the concat chain is deep is caught too. Every `true`/`false`
  // here is a type witness: a recycled header prints a number instead.
  print("${t}${churn(10)}${f}${churn(10)}${s}${churn(10)}${t}${externOne()}${f}${s}\n")
}
